"""One extra practical section per batch 33 guide (general advice; Apple facts stay on the main pages)."""
P = "Apple Maps and Google Maps are separate systems, so a change on one doesn't update the other. "
X = {
"add-business-to-apple-maps": ("A monthly accuracy check", P + "Once a month, open your business in Apple Maps on an iPhone as a customer would. Check that the pin sits on your entrance, the hours match your door sign, the phone number rings through and the website link opens. Compare everything with your Google Business Profile and website. Look in Apple Business for any location showing Updates Available, and read Apple's emails rather than filtering them. If you've changed anything in the last month, such as holiday hours or a new phone system, confirm it's reflected on Apple Maps too. Ten minutes a month prevents the most common problem: a customer driving to a closed business because one map was out of date."),
"apple-maps-place-card": ("How to write the about section", "Keep the about section short, specific and factual. Start with what you do and for whom, such as Family-run bakery making sourdough and custom cakes since 2009. Add what makes you different: languages spoken, certifications, specialties or community ties. Avoid superlatives such as best in Orange County, prices that change often and phone numbers or links, which belong in other fields. Write in the same voice as your website and Google Business Profile description so customers get a consistent picture. If you serve customers in several languages, add the about text in each language you support, keeping the meaning consistent. Review it once a year or whenever your services change, and update it at the same time as your other listings."),
"apple-business-photos": ("A simple photo plan", "Plan photos the way a first-time customer experiences your business. Start outside: a clear daytime shot of the storefront and one of the entrance or parking. Then inside: the main space, the counter or reception, and any seating. Then your work: products, dishes, finished jobs or treatment rooms. Finally, people, with permission: your team at work. Retake photos when you renovate, change signage or add services, and remove ones that no longer match reality."),
"apple-business-showcases": ("A twelve-month showcase calendar", "Map out a year of showcases in one sitting. January: a new-year offer or service. February: Valentine's Day or a quiet-season promotion. Spring: new menu items, products or spring cleaning services. Summer: longer hours, outdoor seating or summer camps. Fall: back-to-school, Halloween or seasonal menus. November and December: holiday hours, gift cards and events. For each month, note the headline, image and action, then create showcases at least three days before each goes live. Align them with your Google Business Profile posts and newsletter so the same message appears everywhere customers look. For Anaheim businesses near the resort area, add showcases around major conventions and events when visitor numbers rise, and check approval status a few days before each one starts."),
"apple-business-actions": ("Matching Actions to your category", "Think about the one thing most customers want to do after finding you. A taqueria with lots of takeout wants Order or Pickup first; a sit-down restaurant wants Reserve; a salon, spa or clinic wants booking; a venue wants Tickets. Make that the Primary Action, then add secondary Actions such as Menu or Delivery under More. Check that each linked page works on a phone, loads quickly and lets customers finish the task without hunting. Review Actions whenever you change booking or ordering providers, because an old link left in place sends customers to a dead end and makes Apple Maps look broken. Compare Action taps in Apple Business insights month to month to see which ones customers actually use. If an Action gets few taps, check whether the linked page is slow or confusing on a phone before removing it, and ask a friend to try booking or ordering from Apple Maps while you watch."),
"apple-business-insights": ("A monthly Apple Maps report", "Keep a simple monthly record with five numbers from Apple Business insights: search taps, the share of category searches, place card views, directions requests and calls. Add the top three zip codes from spatial insights and any showcase or Action taps. Next to each month, note what you changed: new photos, a showcase, updated hours or Actions. After a few months, patterns appear, such as showcases lifting views or new photos increasing directions. Put the same five numbers from your Google Business Profile beside them, so you can see how much each map platform contributes. For a small Orange County business, this one-page report is enough to decide where to spend time each month."),
"apple-branded-mail": ("Checking your email authentication", "Before applying, check that your email is properly authenticated. Ask your email provider or IT person three questions: Is DKIM signing turned on for our domain? Do we have a DMARC record, and what policy does it use? Which other services send email as us, such as booking systems, invoicing tools, website forms and newsletter platforms, and are they DKIM-signed for our domain? Each tool that sends email for you needs to be set up correctly, or some messages won't qualify. Free online DMARC checkers can show whether a record exists, but your provider should confirm the details. Getting this right also helps your email reach inboxes more reliably, even beyond Apple Mail."),
"tap-to-pay-on-iphone-small-business": ("Getting started step by step", "First, check with your current payment service provider whether it supports Tap to Pay on iPhone and which app to use; switching providers isn't always necessary. Second, install the supported app and complete the provider's onboarding, including accepting the Tap to Pay terms. Third, run a few small test transactions with a contactless card and a phone wallet so you're comfortable before using it with customers. Fourth, if you want your logo and name to appear during payment, verify your organization and brand in Apple Business and link the brand to your Tap to Pay merchant ID. Finally, check that your statement descriptor, the name customers see on their card statement, is clear and matches your business name. For mobile businesses across Orange County, these steps take an afternoon and remove the need to carry separate hardware. Keep a backup payment option, such as a payment link sent by text, for the rare customer whose card or phone doesn't support contactless payments, and train any staff who take payments so they can explain the process confidently. Review your payment provider's reports monthly alongside your sales records to catch any problems early."),
"apple-maps-ads": ("Running a sensible test", "Treat your first Apple Maps campaign as a test. Before starting, note your current monthly calls, directions requests and website taps from Apple Business insights, and your bookings or sales from your own records. Run the campaign for one or two months with a modest monthly maximum, promoting the location that most needs customers. Afterwards, compare the same numbers and calculate a rough cost per extra call or visit. If the result is better than your other advertising, increase the budget gradually; if not, improve photos and offers or pause. Keep the place card complete throughout, since ads send people to it. This simple approach avoids spending heavily before you know whether Apple Maps users are a strong audience for your Orange County business."),
}


def add_x(pages):
    for p in pages:
        if p["slug"] in X:
            h, b = X[p["slug"]]
            p["sections"].insert(len(p["sections"]) - 1, {"heading": h, "body_html": f"<p>{b}</p>"})
